#
# Daily Trends – Frontend configuration
#
# Copy to .env.local. EVERY variable is optional: with an empty file the shop
# runs locally with the seeded catalogue, template-generated copy, file-based
# mail delivery and untagged Amazon links. The table in README.md lists what
# happens when each one is unset.
#

# --- Site -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical base URL. Drives metadataBase, canonicals, robots.txt, sitemap.xml
# and the links inside transactional e-mails.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# SQLite file. Relative paths resolve against the repo root. Point this outside
# the repository if it lives in a synced folder (OneDrive/Dropbox) — SQLite's
# file locking is unreliable on those.
# DATABASE_PATH=data/daily-trends.db

# --- Affiliate --------------------------------------------------------------
# Amazon PartnerNet tag. Without it outbound links carry no tag and Amazon pays
# no commission — clicks are still tracked.
# AMAZON_PARTNER_TAG=dailytrends-21

# --- Authentication ---------------------------------------------------------
# Session signing secret. Generate with: npx auth secret
# A hardcoded development fallback is used when unset — set it for anything
# reachable from outside localhost.
# AUTH_SECRET=

# The account registering with this address becomes admin and may open /admin.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=du@example.com

# --- AI (Google Gemini free tier) -------------------------------------------
# Free API key without a credit card: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Unset → npm run ai:descriptions falls back to deterministic German templates.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# Force a provider: gemini | ollama | template
# AI_PROVIDER=

# Fully offline alternative (requires a local Ollama installation).
# OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2

# --- E-mail -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset → messages are written to data/mail-outbox/*.eml and the confirmation
# link is printed to the console, so the double-opt-in flow is testable without
# a mail server. Brevo's free tier (300 mails/day) works as SMTP_HOST.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp-relay.brevo.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# MAIL_FROM=Daily Trends <noreply@daily-trends.local>

# Recipient for contact-form notifications (defaults to ADMIN_EMAIL).
# CONTACT_EMAIL=
